WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.810 --> 00:00:04.640
While it's possible to see a character's death
coming from a mile away, sometimes it comes

00:00:04.640 --> 00:00:06.549
out of nowhere and leaves audiences shocked.

00:00:06.549 --> 00:00:10.650
Other times, there are real-life factors behind
the scenes that end a character's life.

00:00:10.650 --> 00:00:14.710
With that in mind, here's a look at the real
reasons these onscreen lives were ended.

00:00:14.710 --> 00:00:16.240
Han Solo

00:00:16.240 --> 00:00:20.990
Long before the release of 1983's Star Wars:
Return of the Jedi, Harrison Ford began arguing

00:00:20.990 --> 00:00:23.539
that his character, Han Solo, should be killed
off.

00:00:23.539 --> 00:00:27.390
Whether it was from boredom with the famous
space opera or a desire to punch up the narrative,

00:00:27.390 --> 00:00:30.720
Ford has had a death wish for Han Solo for
decades.

00:00:30.720 --> 00:00:35.210
While talking to Conan O'Brien, Ford reiterated
that he wanted Solo to:

00:00:35.210 --> 00:00:42.480
"Sacrifice himself to a high ideal, and give
a little bottom, a little gravitas to the

00:00:42.480 --> 00:00:43.480
enterprise."

00:00:43.480 --> 00:00:48.010
After decades of waiting, Ford finally got
his wish: Han not only died in Star Wars:

00:00:48.010 --> 00:00:52.800
The Force Awakens, but he was killed by his
son, Ben Solo, a.k.a. Kylo Ren.

00:00:52.800 --> 00:00:56.360
According to Abrams, they needed a big moment
in order to have Kylo Ren step out from Darth

00:00:56.360 --> 00:00:57.480
Vader's shadow.

00:00:57.480 --> 00:00:59.860
In a Q&amp;A with Entertainment Weekly, Abrams
said:

00:00:59.860 --> 00:01:02.160
"We knew we needed to do something f---ing
bold.

00:01:02.160 --> 00:01:06.039
The only reason why Kylo Ren has any hope
of being a worthy successor is because we

00:01:06.039 --> 00:01:08.100
lose one of the most beloved characters."

00:01:08.100 --> 00:01:11.149
Well, beloved to everyone except for Harrison
Ford apparently.

00:01:11.149 --> 00:01:18.590
"I argued for thirty years for this to happen,
and finally I wore them down."

00:01:18.590 --> 00:01:19.930
Agent Zed

00:01:19.930 --> 00:01:23.820
Loosely based on the comic series of the same
name, Men in Black follows Will Smith and

00:01:23.820 --> 00:01:27.979
Tommy Lee Jones as they monitor aliens on
Earth and protect the planet from extraterrestrial

00:01:27.979 --> 00:01:28.979
attack.

00:01:28.979 --> 00:01:32.680
Legendary actor Rip Torn appeared in the movie
as Agent Zed, the head of the Men in Black

00:01:32.680 --> 00:01:35.890
organization and reprised his role for the
2002 sequel.

00:01:35.890 --> 00:01:39.950
Unfortunately, his character was killed off
between the second and third installments

00:01:39.950 --> 00:01:43.469
off-screen, and replaced by Emma Thompson
as Agent O.

00:01:43.469 --> 00:01:46.440
"That's just so Zed.

00:01:46.440 --> 00:01:47.700
Thank you."

00:01:48.409 --> 00:01:52.700
Director Barry Sonnenfeld told Io9 that the
reason for Torn's absence was to keep the

00:01:52.710 --> 00:01:56.170
movie from feeling too similar to previous
Men in Black films.

00:01:56.170 --> 00:01:57.170
According to Sonnenfeld,

00:01:57.170 --> 00:01:59.889
"We didn't want to go back to every single
same character.

00:01:59.889 --> 00:02:03.359
[...] We didn't keep Rip Torn as Zed, but
I think we have a new, wonderful leader with

00:02:03.359 --> 00:02:04.359
Emma Thompson."

00:02:04.359 --> 00:02:06.869
While that may be true, there could be more
to the story.

00:02:06.869 --> 00:02:11.739
Around the time Men in Black 3 started principal
photography, Torn was serving a two-year suspended

00:02:11.739 --> 00:02:15.010
sentence for breaking into a bank while heavily
intoxicated.

00:02:15.010 --> 00:02:18.879
Torn's legal troubles might have made it just
too complicated to bring the actor back for

00:02:18.879 --> 00:02:19.879
the sequel.

00:02:19.879 --> 00:02:20.879
Boba Fett

00:02:20.879 --> 00:02:25.439
Boba Fett made his big-screen debut in Star
Wars: The Empire Strikes Back where he cut

00:02:25.439 --> 00:02:28.780
an imposing silhouette as one of Darth Vader's
hired bounty hunters.

00:02:28.780 --> 00:02:33.140
Although he barely spoke a word, he quickly
became a fan favorite, so it was a real surprise

00:02:33.140 --> 00:02:36.490
when he was suddenly killed off at the beginning
of Return of the Jedi.

00:02:36.490 --> 00:02:40.329
According to Craig Miller, the former head
of fan relations for LucasFilm, Boba Fett

00:02:40.329 --> 00:02:42.760
was originally going to be a much bigger villain.

00:02:42.760 --> 00:02:44.299
Talking to Inverse, Miller said that

00:02:44.299 --> 00:02:47.159
"Boba was gonna be the main villain [in the
next film].

00:02:47.159 --> 00:02:51.140
That was the set up, why he was taking Han
Solo away, why there was a thing with him

00:02:51.140 --> 00:02:52.390
in the Christmas special."

00:02:52.390 --> 00:02:56.079
According to Miller, Lucas decided to end
the story with the third movie.

00:02:56.079 --> 00:03:00.360
So, instead of setting up a future fight between
Solo and Fett, Lucas opted just to kill off

00:03:00.360 --> 00:03:01.590
the bounty hunter.

00:03:01.590 --> 00:03:03.409
Col. Steven Hiller

00:03:03.409 --> 00:03:08.150
In the 1996 blockbuster Independence Day,
Will Smith played Colonel Steven Hiller, who

00:03:08.150 --> 00:03:11.820
ends up giving a very memorable welcome to
an invading alien.

00:03:14.000 --> 00:03:15.600
"Welcome to earth!"

00:03:16.260 --> 00:03:20.230
Almost two decades later, Smith is still one
of the biggest movie stars in the world, with

00:03:20.230 --> 00:03:22.490
an almost guaranteed box office draw.

00:03:22.490 --> 00:03:27.219
However, in 2013, when the studio was gearing
up to enter pre-production on the Independence

00:03:27.219 --> 00:03:31.280
Day sequel, Independence Day: Resurgence,
director Roland Emmerich told the New York

00:03:31.280 --> 00:03:34.821
Daily News that he didn't expect Smith to
return, saying he was simply "too expensive"

00:03:34.821 --> 00:03:35.880
to hire.

00:03:35.880 --> 00:03:38.760
"But I ain't mad.

00:03:38.760 --> 00:03:42.320
It's alright, it's alright."

00:03:42.980 --> 00:03:47.319
To explain Smith's non-involvement in the
movie, 20th Century Fox launched an interactive

00:03:47.319 --> 00:03:52.780
website that explained that Colonel Hiller
died during a military test flight in 2007.

00:03:52.780 --> 00:03:53.780
Quicksilver

00:03:53.780 --> 00:03:57.720
Despite enormous success, Marvel Studio's
early movies don't seem to hold much fatal

00:03:57.720 --> 00:04:00.000
danger for our favorite superpowered heroes.

00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:04.700
However, the Marvel Cinematic Universe finally
suffered a casualty in Avengers: Age of Ultron

00:04:04.700 --> 00:04:10.390
when the speedy superhero Quicksilver died
saving Hawkeye and a child from Ultron's attacks.

00:04:10.800 --> 00:04:12.840
"You didn't see that coming."

00:04:16.760 --> 00:04:21.180
At the time, audiences wondered why Quicksilver
was the unlucky Avenger to get killed off.

00:04:21.190 --> 00:04:25.320
As it turns out, there were a couple reasons,
according to Marvel Studios head honcho Kevin

00:04:25.320 --> 00:04:27.430
Feige while talking to MTV News.

00:04:27.430 --> 00:04:34.430
"To add stakes to the ending of the film [...] To
solidify Scarlet Witch's character and Wanda's

00:04:34.430 --> 00:04:36.740
arc in the movie."

00:04:36.740 --> 00:04:40.870
Although the fact that 20th Century Fox had
its own very popular version of Quicksilver

00:04:40.870 --> 00:04:43.690
in the X-Men movies might have been a factor
as well.

00:04:43.690 --> 00:04:45.810
Looks like there's only room for one Quicksilver
in Hollywood.

